    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "According to the World Health Organization, three million deaths worldwide are attributed to obesity. Insulin resistance characterizes approximately half of obese individuals and has been identified as a major risk factor of diseases such as Type II Diabetes Mellitus (T2DM). Therefore, it is important to characterize the biomolecular mechanisms that link adiposity, insulin resistance, and T2DM onset. The advent of new quantitative high-throughput multi-omics technologies now provides opportunities to analyze biomolecular landscapes of complex diseases such as T2DM. In this study, control and insulin resistant patients each underwent a period of weight-gain on a high caloric diet, followed by a period of restricted dieting, and subsequent return to baseline weight. Patient blood samples were then collected at various time points and subjected to longitudinal multi-omics (transcriptomics, proteomics, and glycoproteomics) profiling. A suite of statistical analyses revealed a wealth of blood-based biomolecular signatures associated with insulin resistance and rapid weight-gain. Moreover, machine-learning algorithms then supported the diagnostic power of these biomolecular signatures. Finally, complex novel data types revealed biologically relevant implications on post-translational modifications (in particular, glycosylation) of these signatures. This study not only elucidated potential diagnostic and therapeutic targets in obesity-mediated T2DM, but also matured novel methodologies to produce and analyze multi-omics data comprehensively.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
